# Documented, deliberate exceptions only — see scripts/trivy-scan.sh for how
# this file is used.
#
# challenges/a06-vulnerable-components ships a pinned, ancient lodash
# (4.17.4) specifically to demonstrate OWASP A06. That one dependency alone
# carries a dozen-plus CVEs (plus more from npm's own bundled tooling in the
# base node image), and the list drifts every time Trivy's DB updates — hand
# -maintaining every CVE ID here would be a losing game and would hide real
# signal behind stale entries. So a06 is scanned in *report-only* mode in
# both `make scan` and CI (never gates the build), and is not otherwise
# listed here.
#
# This single entry exists to document the specific CVE the challenge's
# README walks through as the intended exploit, and to show the ignorefile
# mechanism is in use — not to suppress a06's scan results generally.
CVE-2018-3721

# deploy/k8s/base/db.yaml: the official postgres image drops root privileges
# internally via its own entrypoint (gosu) rather than declaring a non-root
# USER, so it can't be forced to runAsNonRoot or a read-only rootfs without
# switching to a custom/non-root postgres image — out of scope for this demo.
# In this repo these two checks only ever fire on that one StatefulSet (every
# other workload sets an explicit hardened securityContext — see
# platform-api.yaml, frontend.yaml, challenges.yaml), so a global ignore is
# safe in practice despite not being path-scoped.
KSV-0014
KSV-0118

# frontend base image (nginx:1.29-alpine-slim): OpenSSL heap use-after-free
# in PKCS7_verify(). Fixed upstream in libcrypto3/libssl3 3.5.7-r0, but as of
# this writing nginx has not republished the alpine-slim image against the
# patched Alpine package (installed version is still 3.5.6-r0) — there is no
# newer tag to move to yet. Not our code, nothing we can do but wait.
# Re-check `docker pull nginx:1.29-alpine-slim && trivy image ...` — once a
# rebuilt image picks up 3.5.7-r0, remove this entry.
CVE-2026-45447
